RESULTS EXPLAINED — Disaster-Aware Insurance Risk Pipeline
==========================================================

Purpose
-------
This document explains the model results for an AI assistant, project report,
or presentation. Use the actual measured results below. Do not claim that every
metric improved or describe correlations as causal effects.

There are two different prediction tasks:
1. Disaster model: binary classification — predicts whether a state has at
   least one FEMA major-disaster declaration the following year.
2. Insurance model: regression — predicts the medical insurance "charges"
   value in the insurance.csv dataset. This is not a classification task.


What changed in the model tuning
-------------------------------
The insurance XGBoost regressor was changed to a shallower, more regularized
configuration:
- 400 trees
- max_depth=2
- learning_rate=0.03
- min_child_weight=5
- reg_lambda=5
- subsample=0.9
- colsample_bytree=0.9

The comparison used the same deterministic RepeatedKFold splits (5 folds x 3
repeats, 15 fold results) before and after tuning. These are cross-validation
averages, not scores on a separate untouched final test set.

Insurance baseline feature set A:

Metric                 Before tuning    After tuning    Direction
MAE                    $2,542.63        $2,483.74       Improved (lower)
RMSE                   $4,591.65        $4,481.64       Improved (lower)
R-squared (R2)          0.8527           0.8579          Improved (higher)

The model's recorded standard deviations across folds after tuning were
approximately $221.84 MAE, $528.41 RMSE, and 0.04 R2. Scores vary between
folds; do not imply that small differences are guaranteed on new data.

Meaning of regression metrics:
- MAE is the average absolute prediction error in the target's units (dollars).
- RMSE is also in dollars and penalizes large errors more strongly than MAE.
- R2 summarizes explained variation relative to predicting the mean; 1 is
  perfect, 0 is equivalent to the mean baseline, and it can be negative.
- "Accuracy" is not reported for this regression task. Do not call R2
  accuracy. A separate accuracy-within-a-dollar-tolerance metric would need to
  be defined and evaluated explicitly.

The final training run recorded the following insurance XGBoost averages:

Feature set            MAE         RMSE        R2
A: baseline            $2,483.74   $4,481.64   0.8579
B: region + risk       $2,482.02   $4,476.82   0.8578
C: risk instead region $2,476.84   $4,471.97   0.8586

The pipeline selects the family by the RMSE of feature set A. XGBoost was
selected. Feature sets B and C have slightly different scores, but their
small differences should not be presented as proof that disaster risk caused
or reliably improves insurance charges.


Disaster classification results
-------------------------------
The disaster model's reported results did NOT change as part of the insurance
regressor tuning. At the default probability threshold of 0.50, the
walk-forward validation results were:

Model                              Accuracy  Precision  Recall  F1     ROC-AUC
Logistic Regression                0.680     0.525      0.210  0.300  0.642
Random Forest (selected)            0.670     0.493      0.360  0.416  0.644
XGBoost                             0.654     0.458      0.330  0.384  0.575
Historical-rate reference           0.667     0.492      0.610  0.545  0.676

The model selection code chooses among learned models by rounded ROC-AUC.
If rounded scores tie, the first model in candidate order is selected. It
does not choose the model by accuracy. Random Forest is the selected final
disaster model in the recorded run.

Metric meanings for classification:
- Accuracy: fraction of all state-years classified correctly at the chosen
  threshold.
- Precision: among predicted disaster years, the fraction that were disaster
  years.
- Recall: among actual disaster years, the fraction the model detected.
- F1: harmonic mean of precision and recall.
- ROC-AUC: ranking/discrimination across thresholds; 0.5 is chance-level.

There is a precision/recall/accuracy trade-off when changing the classification
threshold. For the selected Random Forest at threshold 0.30, accuracy is
approximately 0.578, precision 0.414, recall 0.700, and F1 0.520. Compared
with its threshold-0.50 results, recall and F1 rise, but accuracy and precision
fall. Thus a threshold adjustment does not improve every metric. The saved
reported results use threshold 0.50.

The historical-rate reference has better recall, F1, and ROC-AUC than the
selected Random Forest, while the learned models have different trade-offs.
Report this honestly: the learned model does not clearly beat the reference on
all measures.


Why is disaster-model accuracy only about 67%?
------------------------------------------------
The score is modest because this is a difficult forecasting problem with
limited data and limited predictive information, not because the insurance
regression model has the same accuracy problem.

Evidence from this exact evaluation:
- The walk-forward test set contains 306 state-year examples from 2006-2011:
  100 positive (a disaster occurred the following year) and 206 negative.
- Predicting "no disaster" for every test example would have accuracy
  206 / 306 = 67.3%. This is a simple majority-class baseline, not a useful
  disaster warning system.
- The Random Forest at threshold 0.50 scores 67.0%, essentially the same
  accuracy as that all-negative baseline. It does detect some disaster years
  (recall 0.36), unlike an all-negative classifier (recall 0).
- Across individual test years, the positive rate ranges from 21.6% (2009) to
  43.1% (2007). Year-to-year variation makes a single fixed threshold and
  historical-pattern model less reliable.

Reasons the task is hard:
1. Few examples: the complete panel has 561 rows, but walk-forward testing
   uses only 306 rows. Each row represents one state and one target year.
2. Noisy event outcomes: whether a major disaster declaration occurs in a
   particular state in a particular year is affected by many factors that may
   not be predictable from prior declaration counts.
3. Limited features: the model mostly sees past disaster counts, event
   categories, and event duration/area summaries. It does not have direct
   weather forecasts, climate/exposure measurements, population, or other
   potentially relevant predictors.
4. Strict time-aware evaluation: each test year is predicted using only
   earlier years. This better resembles forecasting the future, but is harder
   than randomly mixing years into training and testing.
5. Small number of test years: six annual test periods provide limited
   evidence about long-term performance.
6. Imbalanced labels: on the test set, about 67.3% of rows are negative.
   Accuracy can therefore look acceptable even for a classifier that misses
   every disaster. Always report recall, F1, and ROC-AUC alongside accuracy.
7. Threshold choice: metrics such as accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 are
   calculated from probabilities using a threshold of 0.50. Lowering it can
   catch more disasters, but the measured example shows that this reduces
   accuracy.

Interpretation: 67% should not be presented as strong predictive accuracy.
It is almost identical to the 67.3% majority-class baseline on the same test
rows. The Random Forest does identify some disaster cases, but its ranking is
modest; the historical-rate reference actually has higher ROC-AUC (0.676
versus 0.644) and recall (0.61 versus 0.36).
More complete event data and meaningful external predictors would be needed
before expecting dependable improvement. Do not promise that adding features
will improve accuracy until they are tested with the same time-aware
validation.


Validation and data limitations
-------------------------------
- Disaster validation is chronological walk-forward validation, testing
  target years 2006 through 2011. It is not a random train/test split.
- The disaster panel has 561 state-year rows and a 35.8% positive rate; the
  event file yields 362 state-level events in the configured date window.
- Event counts in the provided FEMA extract become very sparse after 2012.
  The project therefore treats 2011 as the last reliable year. The displayed
  risk prediction is for 2012, not a current forecast.
- The insurance dataset has 1,338 rows and four broad regions. The
  disaster_risk value is a single fixed value per region, so it contains no
  finer location information than region itself. To test whether disaster
  exposure independently relates to insurance costs, use records with
  state/county/ZIP-level locations and appropriate validation.
- Cross-validation results are estimates from this dataset, not guarantees of
  performance on new populations. The insurance data is commonly used as a
  sample health-insurance charges dataset; predictions should not be described
  as real insurance premiums or financial advice.


Guidance for an AI assistant using these results
------------------------------------------------
When writing a report or answering questions:
1. Separate the disaster classification metrics from the insurance regression
   metrics.
2. Say clearly that insurance MAE, RMSE, and R2 improved after tuning, but
   disaster-model accuracy did not change.
3. Do not say that "all metrics improved." The classification scores did not
   improve in the tuning change, and lowering its threshold trades accuracy
   for recall/F1.
4. Do not call R2 "accuracy."
5. Include the validation method and note the small/truncated disaster data.
6. Do not claim disaster risk causes higher or lower insurance charges; risk
   is only a four-value regional feature in this dataset.
7. If quoting numbers, use the measured tables in this document and explain
   whether a value is a cross-validation average or a threshold-specific
   classification result.
